<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Patch>

  <!-- Fills the AnimalNeck group, modelled on Animal Apparel: Framework's own
       1.6/Patches/Bodies.xml. Same shape, same two-operation dance: one operation adds the
       <li> to bodies whose part already has a <groups> node, a second creates the node for
       the parts that have none. Both carry <success>Always</success>, because a body that
       matches neither is not a failure.

       Two cases, and the second is the reason this file is longer than it looks:

       1. The body HAS a Neck part - every quadruped, plus Bird and Monkey. AnimalNeck goes
          on the neck, where a collar belongs.

       2. The body has NO Neck part - BeetleLike, Snake, TurtleLike and most modded insect
          bodies. Vanilla refuses to equip apparel whose body part group is absent from the
          wearer's body, so without this fallback a collar would simply never be equippable
          on those animals, silently. Dylan's Animal Gear had exactly that hole: it patched
          a Neck group onto //li[def="Neck"] for the quadruped, bird, insect and snake body
          files alike, which was a no-op for the two that have no neck. Here the head takes
          the collar instead.

          The fallback deliberately does NOT put those animals into AnimalHead: that is the
          helmet group, and sharing it would make a collar and a helmet exclude each other.
          It is the same body PART carrying two different groups, which is normal - the
          framework's own patch already puts most parts in more than one.

       contains(def, 'Head') is the framework's own idiom, inherited warts and all: it also
       matches parts like HeadClaw. The consequence is only that such a part counts as
       covered by a collar, which is harmless. -->

  <!-- 1. Bodies that have a neck. -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationAdd">
    <xpath>/Defs/BodyDef[defName != 'Human']//li[def = 'Neck']/groups</xpath>
    <success>Always</success>
    <value>
      <li>AnimalNeck</li>
    </value>
    <order>Append</order>
  </Operation>

  <Operation Class="PatchOperationAdd">
    <xpath>/Defs/BodyDef[defName != 'Human']//li[def = 'Neck' and not(groups)]</xpath>
    <success>Always</success>
    <value>
      <groups>
        <li>AnimalNeck</li>
      </groups>
    </value>
    <order>Append</order>
  </Operation>

  <!-- 2. Bodies that have no neck: the head stands in for it. -->
  <Operation Class="PatchOperationAdd">
    <xpath>/Defs/BodyDef[defName != 'Human' and not(.//li[def = 'Neck'])]//li[contains(def, 'Head')]/groups</xpath>
    <success>Always</success>
    <value>
      <li>AnimalNeck</li>
    </value>
    <order>Append</order>
  </Operation>

  <Operation Class="PatchOperationAdd">
    <xpath>/Defs/BodyDef[defName != 'Human' and not(.//li[def = 'Neck'])]//li[contains(def, 'Head') and not(groups)]</xpath>
    <success>Always</success>
    <value>
      <groups>
        <li>AnimalNeck</li>
      </groups>
    </value>
    <order>Append</order>
  </Operation>

</Patch>
